WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.602
A histogram helps us understand how a numeric variable is distributed.

00:00:04.602 --> 00:00:09.802
The horizontal axis represents numeric ranges, called bins.

00:00:09.802 --> 00:00:15.027
The vertical axis shows how many observations fall inside each range.

00:00:15.027 --> 00:00:19.343
Run the Python code and examine the distribution of order amounts.

00:00:19.872 --> 00:00:21.574
Look for the general shape.

00:00:21.574 --> 00:00:25.214
Is it roughly symmetric? Is it skewed?

00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:27.534
Are there gaps or clusters?

00:00:27.534 --> 00:00:30.844
Is one observation far away from the others?

00:00:30.844 --> 00:00:34.109
Also experiment with the number of bins.

00:00:34.109 --> 00:00:39.674
A histogram is a summary, so the appearance can change when the bin width changes.

00:00:39.674 --> 00:00:45.444
That is why you should not overinterpret very small details in one histogram.

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:52.304
The main purpose is to see the overall distribution and compare what you see with the descriptive statistics from the

00:00:52.304 --> 00:00:53.571
previous slides.
